Visible field (31 repos; categories are based on the one-line descriptions)

- Business operations and customer workflows: 7 — ShopOps, ClearSend, LedgerRoom, Shield, OrderRoom, VesselAI, Full Circle.
- Developer workflow and PR review: 5 — Canary, Preflight, bug-to-PR, PR Pilot, incident-squad.
- Incident response and SRE: 4 — IncidentRoom, Incident Sentinel, SwitchGuard, OpsRelay.
- Knowledge, documentation, and research: 4 — KB Steward, Study Nexus, DocDesk, Context Keeper.
- Security and risk: 3 — DeFi copilot, Evidenceguard, Airlock.
- General team assistants and council rooms: 3 — both CrewPilots, Ranger-Jay.
- Unspecified or demo repos: 5 — Fluxo, Chandanaa, deveshchandiramani, samprati18, victrvondoom.

A judge skimming 200 entries would likely file Flipside under developer workflow/PR review: crowded, with at least five visible neighbors and likely more in the full field. Treaty would likely land under API governance or cross-team business workflow: much less represented in this sample, though still adjacent to developer tooling.

Projected scores (1–10; weighted total out of 10)

- Flipside: Switch 9, technical 7, creativity/impact 8, presentation/documentation 9. Total: 8.25.
- Treaty: Switch 9, technical 7, creativity/impact 9, presentation/documentation 9. Total: 8.50.

Both make Switch meaningfully useful. Flipside has the sharper privacy and addressing-policy fit, but its session seal proves transcript integrity, not that the transcript is genuine. Treaty’s unknown-consumer discovery and removal gate make a memorable business workflow, but its demo depends on access logs with client IDs and working multi-repo CI.

Treaty has the better chance to win Track 01: it is easier to distinguish in a crowded PR and incident field, and its “found consumer blocks removal” moment ties multiple teams’ private work to a concrete business decision. Its biggest risk is that the cross-repo integrations and evidence gates feel staged or don’t work end to end in 3.5 days.

One change that could make Flipside beat Treaty: make the author-approved, verifiable session evidence demonstrably change a real reviewer’s merge decision in a blinded comparison, with a fixture showing the exact limit of what the proof establishes.